WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:13.442
Idea. Walk over a hill. At the very top, for a moment, the path is level. It is not going up or down. On a curve, a point like that is called a stationary point, and the gradient there is zero.

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:25.444
The gradient is d y by d x. So to find stationary points, set d y by d x equal to nought and solve for x. Then put each x into the curve to get y.

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:36.090
For a fraction, there is a shortcut. A fraction equals zero only when its top is zero. So after the quotient rule, set the numerator equal to nought.

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:46.251
So these questions say use calculus to find the stationary points. Give the full coordinates, both x and y, for every stationary point.

00:00:47.360 --> 00:01:00.421
Method. Step one: differentiate. Step two: set d y by d x to nought. For a fraction, set only the numerator to nought, because a fraction is zero only when its top is zero.

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:08.866
Step three: solve for every x. A square gives two answers, plus and minus. Missing one root loses a whole point.

00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:16.895
Step four: put each x into the original curve for y. Never into d y by d x, which just gives nought again.

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:31.545
Worked example. A real question from June twenty twenty one. The curve is y equals x over x squared plus four. Using calculus, find the coordinates of the stationary points. Five marks.

00:01:32.120 --> 00:01:44.557
Step one: differentiate with the quotient rule. u is x, with derivative one. v is x squared plus four, with derivative two x. The top simplifies to four minus x squared.

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:55.829
Step two: set d y by d x to nought. A fraction is zero when its top is zero, so four minus x squared equals nought. The bottom is never zero.

00:01:56.400 --> 00:02:07.609
Step three: x squared equals four gives x equals plus or minus two. A tempting wrong route is to take only x equals two. That misses a whole stationary point.

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:19.313
Step four: put each x into the curve. At x equals two, y is two over eight, a quarter. At x equals minus two, y is minus two over eight, minus a quarter.

00:02:19.880 --> 00:02:28.903
Here is the curve with both points. The top is at two, a quarter. The bottom is at minus two, minus a quarter. This matches the working.

00:02:29.480 --> 00:02:40.775
The exam answer: the stationary points are two, a quarter and minus two, minus a quarter. This explanation is by olevels dot com, not an official mark scheme.

00:02:41.880 --> 00:02:52.459
Common mistake. A common mistake is putting the x values into d y by d x to find y. That just gives nought. The y value comes from the curve.

00:02:53.560 --> 00:03:10.977
Your turn. Now you try, from November twenty twenty. The curve is y equals x cubed minus three x squared minus twenty four x plus six. Use calculus to find the coordinates of each stationary point. Write your answer, then choose Continue to Answer.

00:03:12.080 --> 00:03:27.330
Answer. Differentiate: three x squared minus six x minus twenty four. Set it to nought and take out the common factor three: x squared minus two x minus eight. That factorises to x minus four, times x plus two.

00:03:27.880 --> 00:03:42.187
Put each x into the curve. At four: sixty four, minus forty eight, minus ninety six, plus six, is minus seventy four. At minus two: minus eight, minus twelve, plus forty eight, plus six, is thirty four.

00:03:42.760 --> 00:03:54.811
The most common wrong answer puts the x values into d y by d x, which gives nought, or stops at the x values. Both lose the y marks. Always write full coordinates.

00:03:55.400 --> 00:04:01.222
Remember: differentiate, set to zero, solve for every root, then find y from the curve.
